module bindings.javascript.resolved_url_origin_only_containment;

// A forwarding target has to be resolved before it can be fetched, and the
// WHATWG URL API documents exactly one way to do that with a configured
// base: `new URL(source, base)` resolves the source against the base's
// href. The same API documents the components of the result, and the two a
// containment guard can choose between are not interchangeable: `origin` is
// scheme + host + port and excludes the path, while `href` is the whole
// URL. A guard that containment-checks the resolved URL through `.origin`
// therefore confines where the request goes but not where it lands -- a
// source with an absolute path or leading `..` re-roots the target under
// the same origin, and every path after the base's prefix is reachable
// (CWE-22). Reading `.href` for the comparison is the control: it carries
// the base's path, so prefix containment over it keeps the target inside
// the configured subtree, and its presence anywhere in the function stops
// the report.
//
// The binding reads three atom facts and judges none of them: the URL
// constructor is called (the `call:` token is the last segment, so
// `URLSearchParams` and `URL.createObjectURL` do not match), a binary
// comparison ties a `.startsWith(` call to an `.origin` member operand --
// both substrings sit on the same whitespace-stripped `expr:` token, which
// the frontend derives from the binary-expression node, so the origin read
// has to stand inside the comparison and a comment cannot supply it -- and
// no `.href` read exists. The tie is what separates this from the prefix
// comparisons of ordinary routing: `url.pathname.startsWith("/v1/auth/")`
// compares a parsed request path against a literal route, and an `.origin`
// read elsewhere in the handler does not turn routing into containment.
// All three clauses sit at function scope, which keeps the atoms confined
// to the function that resolves the URL.
//
// Residuals, both in the safe direction. The containment comparison has to
// appear inside a binary expression for the frontend to emit the `expr:`
// token; a guard written `if (!base.startsWith(dest.origin))` with no
// second conjunct emits no token and is not reported. And the control is
// spelled `.href`; a full-URL comparison through `.toString()` suppresses
// nothing, so that correctly-fixed form is still reported.
//
// The judgement lives in the pack rule; this binding only recognises the
// atoms.

binding resolvedUrlOriginOnlyContainment {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "javascript"
    and node.context.call in ["URL"]
    and node.context.expr contains ".startsWith("
    and node.context.expr contains ".origin)"
    and not (node.context.selector contains ".href")
  emit issue code.ResolvedUrlOriginOnlyContainment at node
}
